12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Henderson

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,260
claims of moral desert on the one hand,

14
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:49,080
and of entitlements to legitimate expectations on the other.

15
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:58,540
Rawls argued that it's a mistake to think

16
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:04,400
that distributive justice is a matter of moral desert,

17
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:10,540
a matter of rewarding people according to their virtue.

18
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:16,840
Today we're going to explore that question of moral desert

19
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,960
and its relation to distributive justice,

20
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,340
not in connection with income and wealth,

21
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,560
but in its connection with opportunities,

22
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,160
with hiring decisions and admission standards.

23
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:33,100
And so we turn to the case of affirmative action.

24
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,860
You read about the case of Cheryl Hopwood.

25
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,090
She applied for admission to

26
00:01:42,090 --> 00:01:44,360
the University of Texas Law School.

27
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:49,440
Cheryl Hopwood had worked her way through high school.

28
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,200
She didn't come from an affluent family.

29
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:53,580
She put herself through community college

30
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,780
and California State University at Sacramento.

31
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:01,660
She achieved a 3.8 grade point average there,

32
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:04,380
later moved to Texas, became a resident,

33
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,320
took the law school admissions test,

34
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:09,560
did pretty well on that,

35
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:12,700
and she applied to the University of Texas Law School.

36
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:15,920
She was turned down.

37
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,620
She was turned down at a

38
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:20,140
time when the University of Texas

39
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:23,700
was using an affirmative action admissions policy,

40
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:33,360
a policy that took into account race and ethnic background.

41
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,360
The University of Texas said,

42
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:39,860
40% of the population of Texas is

43
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:44,360
made up of African Americans and Mexican Americans.

44
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:48,360
It's important that we, as a law school,

45
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:52,360
have a diverse student body.

46
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:55,360
And so we are going to take

47
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:58,360
into account not only grades and test scores,

48
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,360
but also the demographic makeup of our class,

49
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:03,360
including our students.

50
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,360
We're going to take into account race and ethnic profile.

51
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:10,360
The result, and this is what Hopwood complained about,

52
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:14,360
the result of that policy is that some applicants

53
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:16,860
to the University of Texas Law

54
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:19,360
School with a lower academic index,

55
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,360
which includes grades and test scores,

56
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:26,360
than hers were admitted, and she was turned down.

57
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:30,360
She said, she argued, I'm just

58
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:32,360
being turned down because I'm white.

59
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:34,360
If I weren't, if I were

60
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:35,360
a member of a minority group,

61
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:36,860
with my grades and test

62
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:38,360
scores, I would have been admitted.

63
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:43,860
And the statistics, the admissions statistics

64
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:46,360
that came out in the trial,

65
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:49,360
confirmed that African American and

66
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:52,360
Mexican American applicants that year

67
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:59,360
who had her grades and test scores were admitted.

68
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:02,360
It went to federal court.

69
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:06,360
Now, put aside the law, let's consider

70
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,360
it from the standpoint of justice and morality.

71
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:12,360
Is it fair or is it unfair?

72
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:17,360
Does Cheryl Hopwood have a case, a legitimate complaint?

73
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:20,860
Were her rights violated by the

74
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:24,360
admissions policy of the law school?

75
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,360
How many say, how many

76
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,360
would rule for the law school

77
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:30,360
and say that it was

78
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,360
just to consider race and ethnicity

79
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:34,360
as a factor in admissions?

80
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:39,860
How many would rule for Cheryl

81
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,360
Hopwood and say her rights were violated?

82
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:48,360
So here we have a pretty even split.

83
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:50,360
All right, now I want to

84
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:52,360
hear from a defender of Cheryl Hopwood.

85
00:04:54,360 --> 00:04:55,360
Yes.

86
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:58,360
You're basing something on that's an arbitrary factor.

87
00:04:58,360 --> 00:04:59,360
You know, Cheryl couldn't control

88
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,360
the fact that she was white

89
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,360
or not in a minority group.

90
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:03,360
She was a minority.

91
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:04,860
And therefore, you know, it's not as

92
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:06,360
if it was like a test score

93
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:07,860
that she worked hard to try

94
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:09,360
and show that she could, you know,

95
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:10,860
put that out there, you know, that

96
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:12,360
she had no control over her race.

97
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:13,360
Good.

98
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:14,360
And what's your name?

99
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:15,360
Bree.

100
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:16,360
Okay, Bree.

101
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:17,360
Stay right there.

102
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:21,360
Now let's find someone who has an answer for Bree.

103
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:23,360
Yes.

104
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:26,360
There are discrepancies in the educational system.

105
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,860
And majority of the time, I

106
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:29,360
know this in New York City,

107
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,360
the schools that minorities go

108
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:31,360
to are not as well funded.

109
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,360
Are not as well supplied as white schools.

110
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:38,360
And so there is going to be a discrepancy naturally

111
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:40,360
between minorities and between whites

112
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,360
if they go to better schools.

113
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:43,860
And they will not do as well on

114
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:45,360
exams because they haven't had as much help

115
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:48,360
because of a worse school system.

116
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:49,360
All right.

117
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:50,360
Let me just interrupt you.

118
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:51,360
Tell me your name.

119
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:52,360
Anisha.

120
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:53,360
Anisha.

121
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,860
Anisha, you're pointing out that

122
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:58,360
minority kids may have gone,

123
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:00,360
in some cases, to a minority school.

124
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,860
And that's because they didn't

125
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:03,360
get the same educational opportunities

126
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:04,860
to schools that didn't give

127
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:06,360
them the same educational opportunity

128
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,360
as kids from affluent families.

129
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:09,360
Yes.

130
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,360
And so the test scores they got

131
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:17,360
may actually not represent their true potential.

132
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,360
Because they didn't receive the same kind

133
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:21,360
of help that they might have received

134
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:23,360
had they gone to a school with better funding.

135
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:24,360
Good.

136
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:25,360
All right.

137
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:26,860
Anisha has raised the point

138
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,360
that colleges still should choose,

139
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,360
for the greatest academic scholarly promise,

140
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:31,860
to take into account the different

141
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:33,360
meaning of the test scores and grades

142
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,360
in the light of educational disadvantage in the background.

143
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:38,360
But in reading the test scores and grades,

144
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,360
they should take into account the different meaning

145
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,360
those tests and grades have

146
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:47,360
in the light of educational disadvantage in the background.

147
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:50,360
So that's one argument

148
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:51,360
in defense of affirmative action.

149
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:52,360
Anisha's argument.

150
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:56,360
Correcting for the effects

151
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:58,360
of unequal preparation.

152
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,360
Educational disadvantage.

153
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,360
There are other arguments.

154
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:07,360
Suppose, just to identify whether

155
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:11,360
there is a competing principle here,

156
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:16,360
suppose there are two candidates

157
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:20,360
who did equally well

158
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:22,360
on the tests and grades,

159
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:26,360
both of whom went to first-rate schools.

160
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,360
Two candidates.

161
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:32,360
Among those candidates,

162
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:35,360
would it be unfair

163
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,360
for the college or university,

164
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:38,360
for Harvard,

165
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:39,360
to say,

166
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,360
we still want diversity

167
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:44,360
along racial and ethnic dimensions,

168
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:47,360
even where we are not correcting

169
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:49,360
for the effects

170
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:52,360
on test scores of educational disadvantage?

171
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:55,360
What about in that case,

172
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,360
Bree?

173
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:58,360
If it's that one thing that puts

174
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:00,360
someone over the edge,

175
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:03,360
then I guess that would be justifiable.

176
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:05,360
If everything else about the individual first,

177
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:08,360
everything they consider about that person's talents

178
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:09,360
and where they come from

179
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:12,360
and who they are without these arbitrary factors

180
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:13,360
is the same.

181
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,360
Without these arbitrary factors, you call it.

182
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,360
But before you were suggesting, Bree,

183
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:20,360
that race and ethnicity

184
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:23,360
are arbitrary factors outside the control of the applicants.

185
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:25,360
True, I would agree with that.

186
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:27,360
And your general principle is that admissions

187
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:30,360
shouldn't reward arbitrary factors

188
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,360
over which people have no control.

189
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:33,360
Right.

190
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:34,360
All right.

191
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:35,360
Who else?

192
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:36,360
Who else would like to?

193
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:37,360
Thank you both.

194
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:39,360
Who else would like to get into this?

195
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:40,360
What do you say?

196
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:41,360
Well, first of all,

197
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:44,360
I'm for affirmative action temporarily,

198
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:46,360
but for two reasons.

199
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:47,360
First of all,

200
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:48,360
you have to look at the university's purpose.

201
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:50,360
It is to educate their students.

202
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:53,360
And I feel that different races,

203
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:55,360
people coming from different races

204
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:56,360
have different backgrounds

205
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:57,360
and they contribute

206
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:00,360
differently to the education.

207
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:01,360
And second of all,

208
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:04,360
when you say they have equal backgrounds,

209
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:06,360
that's not true when you look at the broader picture

210
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:08,360
and you look at slavery.

211
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:09,360
This is kind of a reparation.

212
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:13,360
I think affirmative action is a temporary solution

213
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,360
to alleviate history

214
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:19,360
and the wrongs done to African Americans in particular.

215
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:20,360
And what's your name?

216
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:21,360
David.

217
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:22,360
David.

218
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:23,360
You say that affirmative action is justified,

219
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:24,360
at least for now,

220
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,360
as a way of compensating

221
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:28,360
for past injustice,

222
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,360
the legacy of slavery and segregation.

223
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:31,360
Right.

224
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,360
Who wants to take on that argument?

225
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:36,360
We need now a critic of affirmative action.

226
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:38,360
Yes, go ahead.

227
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:41,360
I think that what happened in the past

228
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:43,360
has no bearing on what happens today,

229
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:45,360
and I think that discriminating based on race

230
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:47,360
should always be wrong,

231
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:48,360
whether you're discriminating against

232
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,360
one group or another.

233
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:52,360
Just because our ancestors did something

234
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:55,360
doesn't mean that that should have any effect

235
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:58,360
on what happens with us today.

236
00:09:58,360 --> 00:09:59,360
All right.

237
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:00,360
Good.

238
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:01,360
I'm sorry, your name is?

239
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:02,360
Kate.

240
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:03,360
Kate.

241
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:04,360
All right.

242
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:05,360
Who has an answer for Kate?

243
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:09,360
Yes.

244
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,360
I just wanted to comment and say that...

245
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:13,360
Tell us your name.

246
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:14,360
My name is Mansour.

247
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:17,360
Because of slavery, because of past injustices,

248
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:20,360
today we have a higher proportion of African Americans

249
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,360
who are in poverty,

250
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,360
who face less,

251
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:25,360
less,

252
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,360
less opportunities than white people.

253
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:29,360
And so because of slavery 200 years ago,

254
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:30,360
and because of Jim Crow,

255
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:32,360
and because of segregation,

256
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,360
today we have injustice based on race.

257
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:37,360
Kate?

258
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:40,360
I think that there are differences, obviously,

259
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:42,360
but the way to fix those differences

260
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,360
is not by some artificial fixing of the result.

261
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:46,360
You need to fix the problem.

262
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:49,360
So we need to address differences in education

263
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:53,360
and differences in upbringing

264
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:54,360
with programs like Head Start

265
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:57,360
and giving more funding to lower income schools

266
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,360
rather than trying to just fix the result

267
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:00,860
so it makes it look like

268
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:02,360
it's equal when really it isn't.

269
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:03,360
Yes.

270
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:06,360
Well, with regard to affirmative action based on race,

271
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:08,360
I just want to say that white people

272
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:10,360
have had their own affirmative action in this country

273
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:11,360
for more than 400 years.

274
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:13,360
It's called nepotism and quid pro quo.

275
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:16,360
So there's nothing wrong with correcting the injustice

276
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:18,360
and discrimination that's been done to black people

277
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:19,360
for 400 years.

278
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:20,360
Good.

279
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:21,360
Tell us...

280
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:22,360
Wait.

281
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:23,360
I'm sorry.

282
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:24,360
Tell us...

283
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:25,360
Wait.

284
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:26,360
Tell us your name.

285
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:27,360
Hannah.

286
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:28,360
Hannah.

287
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:29,360
All right.

288
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:30,360
Who has an answer for Hannah?

289
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:31,360
And just to add to Hannah's point,

290
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:34,360
because we need now someone to respond,

291
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:39,360
Hannah, you could have also mentioned legacy admissions.

292
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:40,360
Exactly.

293
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:41,360
I was going to say,

294
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:42,360
if you disagree with affirmative action,

295
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:43,360
you should disagree with legacy admission

296
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,360
because it's obvious from looking around here

297
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:47,360
that there are more white legacies than black legacies

298
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,360
in the history of Harvard University.

299
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,360
And explain what legacy admissions are.

300
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:53,360
Well, legacy admissions is given to black people.

301
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:54,360
And so,

302
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:56,360
if you disagree with the history of Harvard University,

303
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:57,360
you're giving an advantage to someone

304
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:59,360
who has an arbitrary privilege of their parent

305
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:02,360
having attended the university to which they're applying.

306
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:03,360
All right.

307
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:07,360
So, a reply for Hannah.

308
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:08,360
Yes.

309
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:09,360
In the balcony.

310
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:10,360
Go ahead.

311
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:11,360
First of all,

312
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:15,360
if affirmative action is making up for past injustice,

313
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:17,360
how do you explain minorities

314
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:20,360
that were not historically discriminated against

315
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:23,360
in the United States who get these advantages?

316
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:24,360
Well,

317
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:25,360
I think that the first thing is that

318
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:26,360
we have to be able to argue that affirmative action

319
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:27,360
perpetuates divisions between the races

320
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:28,360
rather than achieve the ultimate goal of race

321
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:29,360
being an irrelevant factor in our society.

322
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:30,360
And tell us your name.

323
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:31,360
Danielle.

324
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:32,360
Hannah.

325
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:33,360
I disagree with that

326
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,360
because I think that by promoting diversity

327
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:35,360
in an institution like this,

328
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:36,360
you further educate all of the students,

329
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:37,360
especially the white students

330
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:38,360
who grew up in predominantly white areas.

331
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:39,360
It's certainly a form of education

332
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:40,360
to be exposed to people from different backgrounds.

333
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:41,360
And you put white students at an inherent disadvantage

334
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:42,360
when you surround them only with the white students.

335
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:43,360
So,

336
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:44,360
I think that the first thing

337
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:45,360
that we have to do is to make sure

338
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:46,360
that we're not just the white students

339
00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:47,360
who are discriminated against

340
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:48,360
by the white students.

341
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:49,360
And I think that's the first thing

342
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:50,360
that we have to do is to make sure

343
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:51,360
that we're not just the white students

344
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:52,360
who are discriminated against

345
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:53,360
by the white students.

346
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,360
And you put white students

347
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:55,360
at an inherent disadvantage

348
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:56,360
when you surround them only with their own kind.

349
00:12:56,360 --> 00:12:57,360
Why should race necessarily

350
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:58,360
be equated with diversity?

351
00:12:58,360 --> 00:12:59,360
There's so many other forms.

352
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:00,360
Why should we assume

353
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:01,360
that race makes people different?

354
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:02,360
Again,

355
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:03,360
that's perpetuating the idea

356
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,360
of racial division

357
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:05,360
within our universities

358
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:06,360
and our society.

359
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:07,360
Hannah.

360
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:10,360
With regard to African American people

361
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,360
being given a special advantage,

362
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:15,360
it's obvious that they bring something special

363
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:16,360
to the table

364
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:17,360
because they have a unique perspective

365
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,360
just as someone from a different religion

366
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:21,360
or socioeconomic background would as well.

367
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:22,360
As you say,

368
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:24,360
there are many different types of diversity.

369
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:26,360
There's no reason that racial diversity

370
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:28,360
should be eliminated from that criteria.

371
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:31,360
Yes, go ahead.

372
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:34,360
Racial discrimination is illegal in this country,

373
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:37,360
and I believe that it was African American leaders

374
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:39,360
themselves when Martin Luther King said

375
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:40,360
he wanted to be judged

376
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:41,360
not on the color of his skin

377
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:43,360
but by the content of his character,

378
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:44,360
his merit, his achievements.

379
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:47,360
And I just think that to decide

380
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:49,360
solely based on someone's race

381
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:51,360
is just inherently unfair.

382
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:53,360
I mean, if you want to correct

383
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,360
based on disadvantaged backgrounds,

384
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:56,360
that's fine,

385
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:58,360
but there are also disadvantaged white people as well.

386
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,360
It shouldn't matter if you're white or white.

387
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:01,360
Tell us your name.

388
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:02,360
Ted.

389
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:03,360
Ted?

390
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:04,360
Yes.

391
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:05,360
Think of Hopwood.

392
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:07,360
It's unfair to count race

393
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:12,360
or I assume you would also say ethnicity or religion?

394
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:13,360
Yes.

395
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,360
Do you think she has a right to be considered

396
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:19,360
according to her grades and test scores?

397
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:22,360
No, there is more to it than that.

398
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:23,360
You need to...

399
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,360
Universities need to promote diversity.

400
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:29,360
So you agree with the goal of promoting diversity?

401
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:31,360
There's ways to promote diversity

402
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:33,360
besides discriminating against people

403
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:36,360
solely based on a factor that they cannot control.

404
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:38,360
All right, so what makes it wrong

405
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:42,360
is that she can't control her race.

406
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:45,360
She can't control the fact that she's white.

407
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:48,360
That's the heart of the unfairness to her.

408
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:50,360
Bree made a similar point,

409
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:54,360
that basing admissions on factors that people can't control

410
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,360
is fundamentally unfair.

411
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:57,360
What do you say?

412
00:14:57,360 --> 00:14:59,360
There's a lot of things you can't control,

413
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:00,360
and if you're going to

414
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:01,360
go through it based on merit,

415
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:03,360
like just based on your test scores,

416
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:05,360
a lot of what you can achieve

417
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:07,360
has to do with the family background that you raised.

418
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:09,360
If both your parents were scholarly,

419
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:11,360
then you have more of a chance

420
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:13,360
of actually being more scholarly yourself

421
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:14,360
and getting those grades.

422
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:15,360
And you can't control what

423
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:16,360
kind of family background you have.

424
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:17,360
You can't control your grades.

425
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:18,360
You can't control what kind

426
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:19,360
of family you were born into.

427
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:20,360
All right, good.

428
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:21,360
That's a great rejoinder.

429
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:22,360
What's your name?

430
00:15:22,978 --> 00:15:23,938
答

431
00:15:23,938 --> 00:15:25,978
Da.

432
00:15:25,978 --> 00:15:30,058
Ted, are you against advantages that

433
00:15:30,058 --> 00:15:31,838
come from the family you were born into?

434
00:15:31,838 --> 00:15:33,718
What about legacy admissions?

435
00:15:33,718 --> 00:15:35,738
I mean, I do believe that

436
00:15:35,738 --> 00:15:37,758
in terms of a legacy admission,

437
00:15:37,758 --> 00:15:40,078
you shouldn't have a special preference.

438
00:15:40,078 --> 00:15:43,118
I mean, there is a legacy admission you could argue

439
00:15:43,118 --> 00:15:44,318
is another part of diversity.

440
00:15:44,318 --> 00:15:45,818
You could say it's important to have

441
00:15:45,818 --> 00:15:47,418
a small percentage of people that

442
00:15:47,418 --> 00:15:52,198
have a several generation family attendance

443
00:15:52,198 --> 00:15:53,598
at a place like Harvard.

444
00:15:53,598 --> 00:15:57,498
However, that should not be an advantaged factor like race.

445
00:15:57,498 --> 00:16:00,478
That should just be another part of promoting diversity.

446
00:16:00,478 --> 00:16:02,158
Should it count at all?

447
00:16:02,158 --> 00:16:04,478
I think that alumni status,

448
00:16:04,478 --> 00:16:06,798
should it count at all, Ted?

449
00:16:06,798 --> 00:16:09,838
Yes, it should count.

450
00:16:09,838 --> 00:16:12,178
All right, I want to step back for a moment

451
00:16:12,178 --> 00:16:13,018
from these arguments.

452
00:16:13,018 --> 00:16:15,258
Thank you all for these contributions.

453
00:16:15,258 --> 00:16:17,498
We're going to come back to you.

454
00:16:17,498 --> 00:16:19,878
If you've listened carefully, I think

455
00:16:19,878 --> 00:16:23,598
you will have noticed three different arguments

456
00:16:23,598 --> 00:16:27,158
emerged from this discussion in defense

457
00:16:27,158 --> 00:16:33,998
of considering race and ethnicity as a factor in admissions.

458
00:16:37,318 --> 00:16:40,098
One argument has to do with correcting

459
00:16:40,098 --> 00:16:44,238
for the effects of educational disadvantage.

460
00:16:47,778 --> 00:16:50,038
That was Anisha's argument.

461
00:16:50,038 --> 00:16:52,697
This is what we might call the corrective argument.

462
00:16:53,598 --> 00:16:57,318
Correcting for differences in educational background,

463
00:16:57,318 --> 00:16:59,018
the kind of school people

464
00:16:59,018 --> 00:17:00,718
went to, the opportunities they had,

465
00:17:00,718 --> 00:17:01,837
and so on.

466
00:17:01,837 --> 00:17:03,417
That's one argument.

467
00:17:03,417 --> 00:17:05,837
What's worth noticing, though, is that that argument

468
00:17:05,837 --> 00:17:10,718
is consistent in principle with the idea

469
00:17:10,718 --> 00:17:14,258
that only academic promise and scholarly potential

470
00:17:14,258 --> 00:17:17,397
should count in admissions.

471
00:17:17,397 --> 00:17:19,308
We just need to go

472
00:17:19,308 --> 00:17:21,218
beyond test scores and grades alone

473
00:17:21,218 --> 00:17:22,818
to get a true estimate of academic success.

474
00:17:22,818 --> 00:17:23,318
Thank you.

475
00:17:23,318 --> 00:17:24,818
And that's the first argument.

476
00:17:24,818 --> 00:17:29,818
Then we heard a second argument that said affirmative action

477
00:17:34,718 --> 00:17:38,078
is justified even where there may not

478
00:17:38,078 --> 00:17:44,158
be the need to correct for educational disadvantage

479
00:17:44,158 --> 00:17:46,938
in a particular applicant's case.

480
00:17:46,938 --> 00:17:51,118
It's justified as a way of compensating for past wrongs,

481
00:17:51,118 --> 00:17:52,538
for historic injustices.

482
00:17:52,538 --> 00:17:56,038
So that's a compensatory

483
00:17:56,038 --> 00:17:59,538
argument, compensating for past wrongs.

484
00:17:59,538 --> 00:18:04,038
Then we heard a third,

485
00:18:04,038 --> 00:18:08,538
a different argument for affirmative action

486
00:18:08,538 --> 00:18:12,538
from Hannah and others that

487
00:18:12,538 --> 00:18:16,538
argued in the name of diversity.

488
00:18:16,538 --> 00:18:19,238
Now, the diversity argument is

489
00:18:19,238 --> 00:18:21,938
different from the compensatory argument

490
00:18:21,938 --> 00:18:28,938
because it makes a certain appeal to the social purpose

491
00:18:28,938 --> 00:18:34,938
or the social mission of the college or university.

492
00:18:34,938 --> 00:18:37,938
There are really two aspects to the diversity argument.

493
00:18:37,938 --> 00:18:41,938
One says it's important to have a diverse student body

494
00:18:41,938 --> 00:18:44,938
for the sake of the educational experience for everyone.

495
00:18:44,938 --> 00:18:47,938
Hannah made that point.

496
00:18:47,938 --> 00:18:49,938
And the other talks about the wider society.

497
00:18:49,938 --> 00:18:50,938
This was the argument made by the university.

498
00:18:50,938 --> 00:18:51,338
Thank you.

499
00:18:51,338 --> 00:18:53,338
This was the argument made by the

500
00:18:53,338 --> 00:18:55,338
University of Texas in the Hopwood case.

501
00:18:55,338 --> 00:18:58,338
We need to train lawyers

502
00:18:58,338 --> 00:19:01,338
and judges and leaders, public officials,

503
00:19:01,338 --> 00:19:07,338
who will contribute to the strength, the civic strength,

504
00:19:07,338 --> 00:19:08,838
of the state of Texas

505
00:19:08,838 --> 00:19:10,338
and the country as a whole.

506
00:19:10,338 --> 00:19:11,838
So there are two different

507
00:19:11,838 --> 00:19:13,338
aspects to the diversity argument.

508
00:19:13,338 --> 00:19:15,838
But both are arguments in

509
00:19:15,838 --> 00:19:18,338
the name of the social purpose

510
00:19:18,338 --> 00:19:20,338
or the social mission or the common good.

511
00:19:20,338 --> 00:19:23,338
Served by the institution.

512
00:19:23,338 --> 00:19:26,338
Well, what about the force of these arguments?

513
00:19:26,338 --> 00:19:30,338
We've also heard objections to these arguments.

514
00:19:30,338 --> 00:19:36,338
The most powerful objection to the compensatory argument is,

515
00:19:36,338 --> 00:19:39,837
is it fair to ask Cheryl

516
00:19:39,837 --> 00:19:43,337
Hopwood today to make the sacrifice,

517
00:19:43,337 --> 00:19:45,837
to pay the compensation for

518
00:19:45,837 --> 00:19:48,338
an injustice that was admittedly committed

519
00:19:48,338 --> 00:19:49,338
and was egregious in the past,

520
00:19:49,338 --> 00:19:52,338
but in which she was not implicated?

521
00:19:52,338 --> 00:19:54,338
Is that fair?

522
00:19:54,338 --> 00:19:56,838
So that's an important

523
00:19:56,838 --> 00:19:59,338
objection to the compensatory argument.

524
00:19:59,338 --> 00:20:02,338
And in order to meet that objection,

525
00:20:02,338 --> 00:20:04,338
we would have to investigate

526
00:20:04,338 --> 00:20:06,338
whether there is such a thing

527
00:20:06,338 --> 00:20:09,338
as group rights or collective

528
00:20:09,338 --> 00:20:12,338
responsibility that reaches over time.

529
00:20:12,338 --> 00:20:14,338
So having identified that issue,

530
00:20:14,338 --> 00:20:18,338
let's set it aside to turn to the diversity argument.

531
00:20:18,338 --> 00:20:22,338
The diversity argument doesn't have

532
00:20:22,338 --> 00:20:26,338
to worry about that question,

533
00:20:26,338 --> 00:20:29,338
about collective responsibility for past wrongs.

534
00:20:29,338 --> 00:20:34,338
Because it says, for reasons Hannah and others pointed out,

535
00:20:34,338 --> 00:20:40,338
that the common good is served, is advanced,

536
00:20:40,338 --> 00:20:47,338
if there is a racially and ethnically diverse student body.

537
00:20:47,338 --> 00:20:50,338
Everyone benefits.

538
00:20:50,338 --> 00:20:54,338
And this indeed was the argument that Harvard made

539
00:20:54,338 --> 00:20:56,338
when it filed a friend of

540
00:20:56,338 --> 00:20:58,338
the court brief to the Supreme Court

541
00:20:58,338 --> 00:21:04,338
in the 1978 case, affirmative action case, the Bakke case.

542
00:21:04,338 --> 00:21:07,338
And the Harvard brief, the Harvard rationale,

543
00:21:07,338 --> 00:21:10,338
was cited by Justice Powell,

544
00:21:10,338 --> 00:21:12,338
who was the swing vote

545
00:21:12,338 --> 00:21:14,338
in the case, upholding affirmative action.

546
00:21:14,338 --> 00:21:17,338
He cited that as providing the rationale that he thought,

547
00:21:17,338 --> 00:21:20,338
was constitutionally acceptable.

548
00:21:20,338 --> 00:21:26,338
Harvard's argument in its brief was this.

549
00:21:26,338 --> 00:21:30,338
We care about diversity.

550
00:21:30,338 --> 00:21:33,338
Scholarly excellence alone has never

551
00:21:33,338 --> 00:21:36,338
been the criterion of admission,

552
00:21:36,338 --> 00:21:39,338
the sole criterion of admission to Harvard College.

553
00:21:39,338 --> 00:21:44,338
Fifteen years ago, diversity meant students from California,

554
00:21:44,338 --> 00:21:46,338
and New York, and Massachusetts.

555
00:21:46,338 --> 00:21:48,838
City dwellers and farm boys,

556
00:21:48,838 --> 00:21:51,338
violinists, painters, and football players,

557
00:21:51,338 --> 00:21:54,338
biologists, historians, and classicists.

558
00:21:54,338 --> 00:21:57,338
The only difference now, Harvard argued,

559
00:21:57,338 --> 00:22:00,338
is that we're adding racial and ethnic status

560
00:22:00,338 --> 00:22:04,338
to this long list of diversity considerations.

561
00:22:04,338 --> 00:22:06,338
When reviewing the large number of candidates

562
00:22:06,338 --> 00:22:10,338
able to do well in our classes, Harvard wrote,

563
00:22:10,338 --> 00:22:12,838
race may count as a plus,

564
00:22:12,838 --> 00:22:15,338
just as coming from Iowa may count,

565
00:22:15,338 --> 00:22:18,338
being a good middle linebacker, or pianist.

566
00:22:18,338 --> 00:22:20,338
A farm boy from Idaho

567
00:22:20,338 --> 00:22:22,338
can bring something to Harvard College

568
00:22:22,338 --> 00:22:25,338
that a Bostonian cannot offer.

569
00:22:25,338 --> 00:22:28,338
Similarly, a black student can usually bring something

570
00:22:28,338 --> 00:22:30,338
a white student cannot offer.

571
00:22:30,338 --> 00:22:34,338
The quality of the educational experience of all students

572
00:22:34,338 --> 00:22:37,338
depends in part on these differences

573
00:22:37,338 --> 00:22:41,338
in the background and outlook that students bring with them.

574
00:22:41,338 --> 00:22:44,338
That was Harvard's argument.

575
00:22:44,338 --> 00:22:46,338
Now what about the diversity argument?

576
00:22:46,338 --> 00:22:49,338
Is it persuasive?

577
00:22:49,338 --> 00:22:51,838
If it's to be persuasive, it

578
00:22:51,838 --> 00:22:54,338
has to meet one very powerful objection

579
00:22:54,338 --> 00:22:57,338
that we've heard voiced here,

580
00:22:57,338 --> 00:23:03,338
by Ted, by Bree.

581
00:23:03,338 --> 00:23:05,338
Unless you're a utilitarian,

582
00:23:05,338 --> 00:23:12,338
you believe that individual rights can't be violated.

583
00:23:12,338 --> 00:23:14,338
And so the question is,

584
00:23:14,338 --> 00:23:18,338
is there an individual right that is violated?

585
00:23:18,338 --> 00:23:21,338
Is Cheryl Hopwood's right violated

586
00:23:21,338 --> 00:23:24,338
if she is used, so to speak,

587
00:23:24,338 --> 00:23:27,338
denied admission,

588
00:23:27,338 --> 00:23:28,838
for the sake of the

589
00:23:28,838 --> 00:23:30,338
common good and the social mission

590
00:23:30,338 --> 00:23:32,338
that the University of Texas

591
00:23:32,338 --> 00:23:34,338
Law School has defined for itself?

592
00:23:34,338 --> 00:23:37,338
Does she have a right?

593
00:23:37,338 --> 00:23:40,338
Don't we deserve to be considered,

594
00:23:40,338 --> 00:23:43,338
according to our excellences, our achievements,

595
00:23:43,338 --> 00:23:45,338
our hard work?

596
00:23:45,338 --> 00:23:49,338
Isn't that the right at stake?

597
00:23:49,338 --> 00:23:53,338
Now we've already heard an answer to that argument.

598
00:23:53,338 --> 00:23:56,338
No, she doesn't have a right.

599
00:23:56,338 --> 00:23:58,338
Nobody deserves to be admitted.

600
00:23:58,338 --> 00:24:00,838
Notice how this gets us back

601
00:24:00,838 --> 00:24:03,338
to the issue of desert versus entitlement.

602
00:24:03,338 --> 00:24:05,838
They're arguing there is no

603
00:24:05,838 --> 00:24:08,338
individual right that Hopwood has.

604
00:24:08,338 --> 00:24:10,338
She doesn't deserve to be admitted

605
00:24:10,338 --> 00:24:12,338
according to any particular set of criteria

606
00:24:12,338 --> 00:24:15,338
that she believes to be important,

607
00:24:15,338 --> 00:24:18,338
including criteria that have only to do

608
00:24:18,338 --> 00:24:20,338
with her efforts and achievements.

609
00:24:20,338 --> 00:24:22,338
Why not?

610
00:24:22,338 --> 00:24:27,338
I think implicit in this argument

611
00:24:27,338 --> 00:24:31,338
is something like Rawls's rejection of moral desert

612
00:24:31,338 --> 00:24:35,338
as the basis of distributive justice.

613
00:24:35,338 --> 00:24:40,338
Yes, once Harvard defines its mission

614
00:24:40,338 --> 00:24:42,338
and designs its admission

615
00:24:42,338 --> 00:24:45,338
policy in the light of its mission,

616
00:24:45,338 --> 00:24:48,338
people are entitled, who fit those criteria,

617
00:24:48,338 --> 00:24:52,338
they are entitled to be admitted.

618
00:24:52,338 --> 00:24:54,338
But according to this argument,

619
00:24:54,338 --> 00:24:57,338
no one deserves that Harvard College

620
00:24:57,338 --> 00:25:01,338
define its mission and design its admission criteria

621
00:25:01,338 --> 00:25:03,338
in the first place in

622
00:25:03,338 --> 00:25:05,338
a way that prizes the qualities

623
00:25:05,338 --> 00:25:07,338
they happen to have in abundance.

624
00:25:07,338 --> 00:25:10,338
Whether those qualities are test scores or grades

625
00:25:10,338 --> 00:25:12,338
or the ability to play the piano

626
00:25:12,338 --> 00:25:15,338
or to be a good middle linebacker

627
00:25:15,338 --> 00:25:17,338
or to come from Iowa

628
00:25:17,338 --> 00:25:20,338
or to come from a certain minority group.

629
00:25:20,338 --> 00:25:26,338
So you see how this debate about affirmative action,

630
00:25:26,338 --> 00:25:28,338
especially the diversity argument,

631
00:25:28,338 --> 00:25:31,338
takes us back to the question of rights,

632
00:25:31,338 --> 00:25:33,338
which in turn takes us back to the question

633
00:25:33,338 --> 00:25:39,338
of whether moral desert is or is not

634
00:25:39,338 --> 00:25:42,338
the basis for distributive justice.

635
00:25:42,338 --> 00:25:44,338
Think about that over the weekend

636
00:25:44,338 --> 00:25:46,338
and we'll continue this discussion next time.

637
00:25:52,338 --> 00:25:56,338
Suppose we're distributing flutes.

638
00:25:56,338 --> 00:25:59,338
Who should get the best ones?

639
00:25:59,338 --> 00:26:01,338
What's Aristotle's answer?

640
00:26:01,338 --> 00:26:03,338
Anyone.

641
00:26:04,338 --> 00:26:06,338
His answer is the best flutes

642
00:26:06,338 --> 00:26:08,338
should go to the best flute players

643
00:26:08,338 --> 00:26:11,338
because that's what flutes are.

644
00:26:12,338 --> 00:26:14,338
And that's what we're for.

645
00:26:20,338 --> 00:26:22,338
When we ended last time,

646
00:26:22,338 --> 00:26:24,838
we were considering arguments

647
00:26:24,838 --> 00:26:27,338
for and against affirmative action,

648
00:26:27,338 --> 00:26:31,338
counting race as a factor in admissions.

649
00:26:31,338 --> 00:26:35,338
And in the course of the discussion,

650
00:26:35,338 --> 00:26:37,338
three arguments emerged,

651
00:26:37,338 --> 00:26:40,338
three arguments for affirmative action.

652
00:26:40,338 --> 00:26:44,338
One of them was the idea

653
00:26:44,338 --> 00:26:47,338
that race and ethnic background should count

654
00:26:47,338 --> 00:26:52,338
as a way of correcting for

655
00:26:52,338 --> 00:26:56,338
the true meaning of test scores and grades.

656
00:26:56,338 --> 00:26:59,338
Getting a more accurate measure

657
00:26:59,338 --> 00:27:02,338
of the academic potential those scores,

658
00:27:02,338 --> 00:27:04,338
those numbers represent.

659
00:27:04,338 --> 00:27:08,338
Second was what we called the compensatory argument.

660
00:27:08,338 --> 00:27:10,338
The argument was that,

661
00:27:10,338 --> 00:27:13,338
the idea of righting past wrongs,

662
00:27:15,338 --> 00:27:17,338
past injustice.

663
00:27:17,338 --> 00:27:20,338
And the third was the diversity argument.

664
00:27:21,338 --> 00:27:23,338
And when Cheryl Hopwood,

665
00:27:23,338 --> 00:27:25,338
in the 1990s,

666
00:27:25,338 --> 00:27:28,338
challenged the University of Texas Law School's

667
00:27:28,338 --> 00:27:30,338
affirmative action program

668
00:27:30,338 --> 00:27:32,338
in the federal courts,

669
00:27:32,338 --> 00:27:34,338
the University of Texas made another version

670
00:27:34,338 --> 00:27:36,338
of the diversity argument,

671
00:27:36,338 --> 00:27:39,338
saying that the broader social purpose,

672
00:27:39,338 --> 00:27:43,338
the social mission of the University of Texas Law School,

673
00:27:43,338 --> 00:27:46,338
is to produce leaders

674
00:27:46,338 --> 00:27:48,338
in the legal community,

675
00:27:48,338 --> 00:27:50,338
in the political community,

676
00:27:50,338 --> 00:27:51,338
among judges,

677
00:27:51,338 --> 00:27:52,338
lawyers,

678
00:27:52,338 --> 00:27:54,338
legislators,

679
00:27:54,338 --> 00:28:00,338
and therefore it's important that we produce leaders

680
00:28:00,338 --> 00:28:05,338
who reflect the background and the experience

681
00:28:05,338 --> 00:28:07,338
and the ethnic and the racial composition

682
00:28:07,338 --> 00:28:08,338
of the state of Texas.

683
00:28:09,338 --> 00:28:14,338
It's important for serving our wider social mission.

684
00:28:14,338 --> 00:28:18,338
That was the University of Texas Law School's argument.

685
00:28:18,338 --> 00:28:23,338
And then we considered an objection

686
00:28:23,338 --> 00:28:25,338
to the diversity argument,

687
00:28:25,338 --> 00:28:28,338
which after all is an argument in the name of

688
00:28:28,338 --> 00:28:30,338
the social mission,

689
00:28:30,338 --> 00:28:33,338
the common good.

690
00:28:33,338 --> 00:28:38,338
We saw that Rawls does not simply believe

691
00:28:38,338 --> 00:28:40,338
that arguments of the common good

692
00:28:40,338 --> 00:28:42,338
or the general welfare should prevail

693
00:28:42,338 --> 00:28:45,338
if individual rights must be violated

694
00:28:45,338 --> 00:28:48,338
in the course of promoting the common good.

695
00:28:48,338 --> 00:28:50,338
You remember that was the question,

696
00:28:50,338 --> 00:28:52,338
the challenge,

697
00:28:52,338 --> 00:28:55,338
to the diversity rationale that we were considering

698
00:28:55,338 --> 00:28:58,338
when we finished last time.

699
00:29:00,338 --> 00:29:02,338
And we began to discuss the question,

700
00:29:02,338 --> 00:29:05,338
well, what right might be at stake?

701
00:29:05,338 --> 00:29:07,338
Maybe the right to be considered,

702
00:29:08,338 --> 00:29:15,338
according to factors within one's control.

703
00:29:15,338 --> 00:29:18,338
Maybe this is the argument that Cheryl Hopwood

704
00:29:18,338 --> 00:29:21,338
implicitly was making.

705
00:29:21,338 --> 00:29:25,338
She can't help the fact that she's white.

706
00:29:25,338 --> 00:29:29,338
Why should her chance at getting into law school

707
00:29:29,338 --> 00:29:32,338
depend on a factor she can't control?

708
00:29:34,338 --> 00:29:36,338
And then Hannah,

709
00:29:36,338 --> 00:29:38,338
who was advancing an argument,

710
00:29:38,338 --> 00:29:40,338
last time,

711
00:29:40,338 --> 00:29:41,338
said,

712
00:29:41,338 --> 00:29:43,338
Harvard has the right

713
00:29:43,338 --> 00:29:45,338
to define its mission

714
00:29:45,338 --> 00:29:46,338
any way it wants to.

715
00:29:46,338 --> 00:29:49,338
It's a private institution.

716
00:29:49,338 --> 00:29:54,338
And it's only once Harvard defines its mission

717
00:29:54,338 --> 00:29:58,338
that we can identify

718
00:29:58,338 --> 00:30:01,338
the qualities that count.

719
00:30:01,338 --> 00:30:04,338
So no rights are being violated.

720
00:30:04,338 --> 00:30:06,338
Now,

721
00:30:06,338 --> 00:30:08,338
what about that argument?

722
00:30:08,338 --> 00:30:11,338
What I would like to do is to hear objections

723
00:30:11,338 --> 00:30:13,338
to that reply,

724
00:30:13,338 --> 00:30:16,338
and then see whether others have an answer.

725
00:30:16,338 --> 00:30:18,338
Yes.

726
00:30:18,338 --> 00:30:20,338
And tell us your name.

727
00:30:20,338 --> 00:30:21,338
Da.

728
00:30:21,338 --> 00:30:22,338
Da.

729
00:30:22,338 --> 00:30:23,338
Right.

730
00:30:23,338 --> 00:30:24,338
You spoke up last time.

731
00:30:24,338 --> 00:30:25,338
All right.

732
00:30:25,338 --> 00:30:26,338
How do you answer that argument?

733
00:30:26,338 --> 00:30:28,338
Well, I think there was two things in there.

734
00:30:28,338 --> 00:30:30,338
One of them was that a private institution

735
00:30:30,338 --> 00:30:32,338
could define its mission however it wants.

736
00:30:32,338 --> 00:30:35,338
But then that doesn't make however it defines it right.

737
00:30:35,338 --> 00:30:37,338
Like, I could define my personal mission as,

738
00:30:37,338 --> 00:30:39,338
I want to collect all the money in the world.

739
00:30:39,338 --> 00:30:41,338
But does that make it even a good mission?

740
00:30:41,338 --> 00:30:42,338
So you can't, like,

741
00:30:42,338 --> 00:30:43,338
you can't say that just because

742
00:30:43,338 --> 00:30:45,338
a college is a private institution,

743
00:30:45,338 --> 00:30:47,338
it could just define it whatever it wants.

744
00:30:47,338 --> 00:30:48,338
We still have to think about

745
00:30:48,338 --> 00:30:50,338
what are the ways defining it is right.

746
00:30:50,338 --> 00:30:53,338
And in the case of affirmative action,

747
00:30:53,338 --> 00:30:54,338
a lot of people have said that

748
00:30:54,338 --> 00:30:57,338
since there's a lot of other factors involved,

749
00:30:57,338 --> 00:30:58,338
we could, why not race?

750
00:30:58,338 --> 00:31:00,338
Like, if we already know that the system is imperfect.

751
00:31:00,338 --> 00:31:01,338
All right.

752
00:31:01,338 --> 00:31:03,338
I want to stick with your first point, Da.

753
00:31:03,338 --> 00:31:05,338
Here's Da's objection.

754
00:31:05,338 --> 00:31:07,338
Can a college,

755
00:31:07,338 --> 00:31:08,338
or university,

756
00:31:08,338 --> 00:31:11,338
define its social purpose any way it wants to,

757
00:31:11,338 --> 00:31:19,338
and then define admissions criteria accordingly?

758
00:31:19,338 --> 00:31:21,338
What about the University of Texas Law School,

759
00:31:21,338 --> 00:31:23,338
not today,

760
00:31:23,338 --> 00:31:27,338
but in the 1950s?

761
00:31:27,338 --> 00:31:31,338
Then there was another Supreme Court case

762
00:31:31,338 --> 00:31:33,338
against the admissions policy

763
00:31:33,338 --> 00:31:36,338
of the University of Texas Law School,

764
00:31:36,338 --> 00:31:37,338
because it was segmented

765
00:31:37,338 --> 00:31:39,338
and segregated.

766
00:31:39,338 --> 00:31:42,338
It only admitted whites.

767
00:31:42,338 --> 00:31:44,338
And when the case went

768
00:31:44,338 --> 00:31:46,338
to court back in the 50s,

769
00:31:46,338 --> 00:31:48,338
the University of Texas Law School

770
00:31:48,338 --> 00:31:51,338
also invoked its mission.

771
00:31:51,338 --> 00:31:54,338
Our mission as a law school

772
00:31:54,338 --> 00:31:57,338
is to educate lawyers

773
00:31:57,338 --> 00:31:58,338
for the Texas bar,

774
00:31:58,338 --> 00:32:00,338
for Texas law firms.

775
00:32:00,338 --> 00:32:03,338
And no Texas law firm

776
00:32:03,338 --> 00:32:06,338
hires African Americans.

777
00:32:06,338 --> 00:32:08,338
So to fulfill our mission,

778
00:32:08,338 --> 00:32:10,338
we only admit whites.

779
00:32:13,338 --> 00:32:15,338
Or consider Harvard

780
00:32:15,338 --> 00:32:17,338
in the 1930s,

781
00:32:18,338 --> 00:32:20,338
when it had

782
00:32:20,338 --> 00:32:23,338
anti-Jewish quotas.

783
00:32:23,338 --> 00:32:25,338
President Lowell,

784
00:32:25,338 --> 00:32:28,338
president of Harvard in the 1930s, said

785
00:32:30,338 --> 00:32:34,338
that he had nothing personally against Jews,

786
00:32:34,338 --> 00:32:36,338
but he invoked the mission,

787
00:32:36,338 --> 00:32:38,338
the social purpose of Harvard,

788
00:32:38,338 --> 00:32:39,338
he said,

789
00:32:39,338 --> 00:32:43,338
which is not only to train intellectuals.

790
00:32:43,338 --> 00:32:45,338
Part of the mission of Harvard,

791
00:32:45,338 --> 00:32:46,338
he said,

792
00:32:46,338 --> 00:32:48,338
is to train stockbrokers for Wall Street,

793
00:32:48,338 --> 00:32:50,338
presidents and senators.

794
00:32:50,338 --> 00:32:52,338
And there are very few Jews

795
00:32:52,338 --> 00:32:55,338
who go into those professions.

796
00:32:55,338 --> 00:32:59,338
Now, here's the challenge.

797
00:32:59,338 --> 00:33:02,338
Is there a principle distinction

798
00:33:02,338 --> 00:33:05,338
between the invocation

799
00:33:05,338 --> 00:33:07,338
of the social purpose

800
00:33:07,338 --> 00:33:10,338
of the college or university today

801
00:33:10,338 --> 00:33:13,338
in the diversity rationale

802
00:33:13,338 --> 00:33:15,338
and the invocation of the social purpose

803
00:33:15,338 --> 00:33:17,338
or mission of the university

804
00:33:17,338 --> 00:33:20,338
by Texas in the 1950s

805
00:33:20,338 --> 00:33:22,338
or Harvard in the 1930s?

806
00:33:22,338 --> 00:33:25,338
Is there a difference in principle?

807
00:33:25,338 --> 00:33:27,338
What's the reply?

808
00:33:29,338 --> 00:33:30,338
Hannah?

809
00:33:30,338 --> 00:33:31,338
Well, I think that the principle is,

810
00:33:31,338 --> 00:33:32,338
basically,

811
00:33:32,338 --> 00:33:34,338
the distinction between inclusion versus exclusion.

812
00:33:34,338 --> 00:33:37,338
I think that it's morally wrong of the university

813
00:33:37,338 --> 00:33:38,338
to say,

814
00:33:38,338 --> 00:33:40,338
we're going to exclude you

815
00:33:40,338 --> 00:33:42,338
on the basis of your religion or your race.

816
00:33:42,338 --> 00:33:44,338
That's denial on the basis of arbitrary factors.

817
00:33:44,338 --> 00:33:46,338
What Harvard is trying to do today

818
00:33:46,338 --> 00:33:48,338
with its diversity initiatives

819
00:33:48,338 --> 00:33:51,338
is to include groups that were excluded in the past.

820
00:33:51,338 --> 00:33:52,338
Good.

821
00:33:52,338 --> 00:33:53,338
Let's see if,

822
00:33:53,338 --> 00:33:54,338
stay there.

823
00:33:54,338 --> 00:33:57,338
Let's see if someone would like to reply.

824
00:33:57,338 --> 00:33:58,338
So,

825
00:33:58,338 --> 00:34:00,338
I think that the principle

826
00:34:00,338 --> 00:34:27,618
of the

827
00:34:27,618 --> 00:34:28,338
Good.

828
00:34:28,338 --> 00:34:30,338
So, the element of malice

829
00:34:30,338 --> 00:34:34,338
is that in the historic segregationist,

830
00:34:34,338 --> 00:34:39,338
racist, anti-Semitic quotas or prohibitions,

831
00:34:39,338 --> 00:34:41,338
there was built into them

832
00:34:41,338 --> 00:34:43,338
a certain kind of malice,

833
00:34:43,338 --> 00:34:45,338
a certain kind of judgment

834
00:34:45,338 --> 00:34:49,338
that African Americans or Jews

835
00:34:49,338 --> 00:34:53,338
were somehow less worthy than everybody else.

836
00:34:53,338 --> 00:34:57,338
Whereas present day affirmative action programs

837
00:34:57,338 --> 00:34:57,618
don't involve or imply any such,

838
00:34:57,618 --> 00:35:01,618
judgment.

839
00:35:01,618 --> 00:35:03,618
What it amounts to saying is,

840
00:35:03,618 --> 00:35:08,618
so long as a policy

841
00:35:08,618 --> 00:35:12,618
just uses people in a way

842
00:35:12,618 --> 00:35:16,618
as valuable to the social purpose

843
00:35:16,618 --> 00:35:18,618
of the institution,

844
00:35:18,618 --> 00:35:19,618
it's okay,

845
00:35:19,618 --> 00:35:21,618
provided it doesn't judge them

846
00:35:21,618 --> 00:35:24,618
maliciously as Stevie might add,

847
00:35:24,618 --> 00:35:26,618
as intrinsically less worthy.

848
00:35:27,618 --> 00:35:30,618
I'd like to raise a question.

849
00:35:30,618 --> 00:35:33,618
Doesn't that concede

850
00:35:33,618 --> 00:35:35,618
that all of us,

851
00:35:35,618 --> 00:35:38,618
when we compete for positions,

852
00:35:38,618 --> 00:35:41,618
